# HakiOS fragment: the Qualcomm SM8750 family (sun) -- what is true of the SoC, whatever device it is in.
#
# Deliberately minimal: none of the SoC drivers of the reference family (SC7280) has been brought
# up on an SM8750, and their addresses are the SC7280's -- CPUFREQ (the big core's clocks),
# THERMAL (the sensors at tm0/tm1), SOCINFO and PMIC are all left to a device's own fragment to
# turn on once each has been made to work here. What IS known of this family and no other:
#
#   - the cores: eight, in two clusters with no uniform MPIDR shift (0x000-0x500, then
#     0x10000-0x10100); the loader's platform table (xnuloader/loader.c, platform_dodge) numbers
#     them for the kernel;
#   - the GIC's redistributor window is 2 MB (a 0x40000 stride per core, not the kernel's 0x20000);
#     the loader patches the kernel's walk of it while loading;
#   - the PMIC arbiter is version 7 with its own layout (core and APID table at 0x0c400000,
#     observer at 0x0c440000 with 0x20 per APID, write side "chnls" at 0x0c500000 with 0x1000 per
#     APID; up to 1024 APIDs -- this phone has 896): drivers/input/hakios.buttons.c (arb_v8) and
#     this phone's touch driver know it;
#   - the audio DSP (and the compute DSP) have a device tree of their own, a second signed image
#     with its own PAS id started before the DSP itself (drivers/audio/hakios.adsp.c, adsp_dtb=);
#   - the AOP command database and the shared memory are where the phone's own device tree says
#     (aop_cmd_db_region 0x81c60000, smem_region 0x81d00000 on this phone; not the reference
#     phone's, which reading aborts): devices/<name>/soc.args carries each phone's.
#
# The addresses above are the family's; a phone's own are in its soc.args (tools/soc-args.py).
CONFIG_SOC="sun"
